WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:03.770
With our HLOD system now explained,

2
00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:06.809
that is going to bring us to the end of this video tutorial

3
00:00:06.809 --> 00:00:07.330
series.

4
00:00:07.330 --> 00:00:09.130
Now, in this video tutorial series,

5
00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:12.449
we first started talking about optimization philosophies.

6
00:00:12.449 --> 00:00:14.970
Remember, optimization is an ongoing process, something

7
00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:17.370
that you will do throughout the lifetime of your project.

8
00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:19.570
We then talked about optimizations that

9
00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:21.570
can take place at project setup.

10
00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:23.070
One of the things to remember here

11
00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:25.070
is that anything you do in your project setup

12
00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:28.210
will often require you restart the editor in order

13
00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:29.929
to see that change take place.

14
00:00:29.969 --> 00:00:32.570
We then took a look at texture optimizations.

15
00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:35.969
Remember, textures need to remain in a power of 2

16
00:00:35.969 --> 00:00:39.130
in the x or the y axis to get the most benefits out

17
00:00:39.130 --> 00:00:42.450
of Unreal's built-in texture streaming technology.

18
00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:44.829
We then took a look at ways to optimize

19
00:00:44.829 --> 00:00:47.049
our static and skeletal meshes.

20
00:00:47.049 --> 00:00:49.409
Remember, when you're dealing with static meshes that

21
00:00:49.409 --> 00:00:51.670
are non-Nanite based, you will

22
00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:54.090
need to create level of details for yourself

23
00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:55.570
using the built-in tools.

24
00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:58.130
Nanite does this for you automatically.

25
00:00:58.130 --> 00:01:01.689
We also looked at ways we can apply LODs

26
00:01:01.689 --> 00:01:04.650
to our skeletal meshes using Unreal's built-in level

27
00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:05.769
of detail tools.

28
00:01:05.769 --> 00:01:08.530
And remember, we can do this, and it will still

29
00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:11.930
optimize our asset while keeping our animations properly

30
00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:12.890
scanned.

31
00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:16.370
We then took a look at how we can optimize our materials

32
00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:19.209
using things like the parent material workflow,

33
00:01:19.209 --> 00:01:23.439
as well as adding static switches and feature parameter

34
00:01:23.439 --> 00:01:27.120
switches to help make sure that our materials perform

35
00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:30.180
and look as they expect on our target platform.

36
00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:32.680
We then took a look at some of the optimization of the modes

37
00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:35.680
Unreal Engine 5 provides to help us visually

38
00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:40.879
debug our levels lighting, texturing, and quad overdraw.

39
00:01:40.879 --> 00:01:43.719
And then we took a look at a number of optimization tools

40
00:01:43.719 --> 00:01:46.439
like Unreal's console command help section,

41
00:01:46.439 --> 00:01:51.159
as well as Unreal's mesh merging and hierarchical level

42
00:01:51.159 --> 00:01:54.920
of detail system that help you procedurally

43
00:01:54.920 --> 00:02:00.159
make your meshes bind so that you can run projects faster

44
00:02:00.159 --> 00:02:02.280
and more efficiently on whatever platform

45
00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:05.150
you happen to be developing for.

46
00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:07.870
Again, I want to thank you so much for following along

47
00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:09.590
with me in this video tutorial series,

48
00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:12.030
and I'll see you next time.
